{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Narrative is the recurring first visible point of editorial hierarchy drift",
      "description": "Across Experiments 007–009 the Narrative stage is the most frequent earliest stage where a company-first editorial hierarchy breaks, introducing unsupported audiences, stronger factual claims, or an orchestration-centered through-line that displaces the intended company introduction.",
      "audience": "Content workflow owners, editorial leads, and experiment designers working on Nuëyon.Compose pipelines",
      "rationale": "This is the strongest, best-supported single editorial opportunity because it identifies a concrete locus where upstream intentions (company-first) most often fail in tangible, reproducible ways. Focusing interventions at Narrative (rather than only Idea or Compose) targets the stage that most commonly converts a permissible technical description into a higher-risk editorial claim or hierarchy inversion.",
      "evidence": "Experiment report summary: Narrative is the earliest observable hierarchy shift in multiple runs (007 runs 001–002, 009 baseline 002/003, and candidates 001–003). Cross-experiment patterns state “Narrative is the most recurrent first visible hierarchy shift.” Specific examples: Experiment 007 candidate 001 turns Synthesis’s orchestration detail into a through-line at Narrative; Experiment 009 candidate runs show Narrative inventing founders/operators and anchoring public identity in orchestration. The report’s methodology explicitly localizes first visible drift by stage, with Narrative listed most often."
    },
    {
      "title": "A company-first Idea selection is necessary but not sufficient — downstream stages must be constrained/tested",
      "description": "Experiment 009 demonstrates that selecting a company-first Idea did not prevent downstream stages (Synthesis, Narrative, Compose) from reframing the story toward orchestration or adding unsupported claims; therefore experiments and controls must target downstream stage behavior, not only Idea selection.",
      "audience": "Experiment designers, product communicators, and prompt engineering teams planning pipeline trials",
      "rationale": "This idea matters because it reframes experimental strategy: changing only Idea prompts will not reliably preserve editorial hierarchy. The evidence supports designing controlled trials that hold Idea constant while testing Narrative (and possibly Synthesis) boundaries to understand where and why drift occurs.",
      "evidence": "Executive findings: Experiment 009 shows all six baseline/candidate runs kept Nuëyon primary in Idea but issues appear downstream (orchestration-centered framing, unsupported audience/validation/outcome language). The report explicitly: “Experiment 009 shows that a company-first selected Idea is not sufficient.” The implication section recommends a controlled upstream-boundary trial targeting Narrative with held constants."
    },
    {
      "title": "Format references leaked into upstream prompts — remove format priming from Idea/Research/Narrative",
      "description": "The Master Workflow and frozen prompt snapshots include explicit format references (Article, LinkedIn, supported output formats) in Idea, Research, and Narrative prompts, creating an instruction-boundary inconsistency that plausibly primes downstream stages toward platform-oriented framing.",
      "audience": "Prompt architects, workflow governance teams, and those responsible for Master Workflow design",
      "rationale": "While causation is not proven, the presence of explicit format references upstream is a low-risk, high-reward fix: enforcing true format-independence upstream removes a plausible source of premature format-driven story choices and keeps Compose responsible for platform mechanics.",
      "evidence": "Format-agnostic architecture audit: frozen Idea system prompt lists “supported output formats” and calls the agent an article writer; Research and Narrative prompts also reference article/LinkedIn compatibility. The report notes this as an “instruction-boundary inconsistency” and says it is a plausible source of format priming, though it does not demonstrate causation."
    },
    {
      "title": "Compose amplifies or crystallizes upstream language into stronger factual claims — treat Compose as an amplifier in quality gates",
      "description": "Compose sometimes converts upstream wording into stronger outcome or certainty statements (e.g., “repeatable process,” “predictable and repeatable,” “orchestration-first”), meaning final-stage controls are necessary to prevent factual overclaims even when earlier stages largely inherited a frame.",
      "audience": "Compose engineers, output editors, and QA/evaluation teams",
      "rationale": "Identifying Compose as an amplifier creates a targeted editorial control point: either constrain Compose’s factual strengthening or add a gate that flags amplified claims. This is actionable because Compose outputs are the last step before published text and already show the largest claim escalations in some runs.",
      "evidence": "Multiple run examples where Compose adds stronger language: Experiment 007 candidate 002 — Compose adds “predictable and repeatable”; Experiment 009 baseline 001 — Compose reports a “repeatable process that reliably converts” content (a factual overclaim). The report notes Compose can “amplify, add factual strength, or simply inherit” upstream frames."
    },
    {
      "title": "Editorial quality (engagement, hook, readability) is under-measured and should be added as a separate gate",
      "description": "Existing evaluations emphasize factuality, hierarchy, and condition fidelity but do not consistently measure opening pull, narrative progression, readability, or memorability—areas where surviving company-first text can still be weak.",
      "audience": "Evaluation designers, editors, and product communicators responsible for content quality metrics",
      "rationale": "Adding a consistent editorial-quality rubric complements factual checks and hierarchy gates, increasing the chance that preserved company-first outputs are not only source-aligned but also compelling and platform-ready.",
      "evidence": "Cross-experiment patterns observation: “Engagement has been under-measured.” The report states earlier run evaluations mainly score factuality, hierarchy, and condition fidelity and lack a consistent hook/readability/attention rubric. It recommends that the next trial separately evaluate factual claims, company-first story hierarchy, and editorial quality (opening interest, clarity, narrative progression, concise readable prose)."
    }
  ]
}

